Step-by-Step Guide: Configuring Password Policy in Active Directory
Purpose

Password Policy is used to enforce strong password rules across a domain.
This helps improve security by requiring users to create stronger passwords and change them according to defined standards.

Step 1: Open Group Policy Management
On the Windows Server machine:
Open Server Manager

Click Tools
Select Group Policy Management
This opens the Group Policy Management Console (GPMC), where domain policies can be created and managed.

![Open Group Policy Console](Screenshot/open-gpo-console.png)

Step 2: Expand the Domain
In the left pane of Group Policy Management:
Expand Forest
Expand Domains
Select your domain
This allows you to choose where you want to create and link the new Group Policy Object.

![Group Policy Management Console](Screenshot/gpo-management-console.png)

Step 3: Create a New Group Policy Object
Right-click the domain or selected Organizational Unit (OU), then click:
Create a GPO in this domain, and Link it here
This creates a new Group Policy Object and links it to the selected location.

![Create GPO](Screenshot/create-gpo.jpg)

Step 4: Name the GPO
When the New GPO dialog box appears:
Enter the name:

Password Policy
Click OK

The new Group Policy Object will now appear under the selected domain or OU.

![Name Password Policy](Screenshot/name-password-policy.jpg)

Step 5: Edit the Password Policy GPO
Right-click the newly created Password Policy GPO and select:
Edit
This opens the Group Policy Management Editor, where the policy settings can be configured.

![Open Password Policy Editor](Screenshot/open-pp-editor.jpg)

Step 6: Navigate to Password Policy Settings
In the Group Policy Management Editor, go to:
Computer Configuration → Policies → Windows Settings → Security Settings → Account Policies → Password Policy
This section contains the domain password settings.

![Select Password Policy](Screenshot/select-password-policy.jpg)

Step 7: Configure Minimum Password Length
Double-click:
Minimum password length

Then:
Check Define this policy setting
Enter the minimum number of characters

In my lab, I set it to:
12 characters
Click Apply
Click OK
This ensures users must create passwords with at least 12 characters.

![Security Settings](Screenshot/security-settings.jpg)

Step 8: Configure Password Complexity
Double-click:
Password must meet complexity requirements
Then:
Check Define this policy setting
Select Enabled
Click Apply
Click OK

This forces users to create more secure passwords using a combination of uppercase, lowercase, numbers, and special characters.

![Password Enabled](Screenshot/enabled-password.jpg)

Step 9: Configure Password Age Settings
Still inside Password Policy, configure the password age values.
In my lab, I used:
Maximum password age: 90 days
Minimum password age: 30 days

These settings help control how often passwords can and must be changed.

![Final Password Policy](Screenshot/final-password-policy.PNG)

Step 10: Verify the Final Configuration
After completing the settings, review the Password Policy window to confirm the policy values have been applied.
Final configured values in this lab:

Setting	Value
Minimum Password Length	12 characters
Maximum Password Age	90 days
Minimum Password Age	30 days
Password Complexity	Enabled

This confirms that the Password Policy has been successfully configured.

![Final Password Policy](Screenshot/final-password-policy.jpg)

Result
The Password Policy now enforces:
Stronger password creation
Password complexity requirements
Defined password expiration rules
This improves domain security and reduces the risk of weak passwords.
Additional Policies Implemented in This Lab

In addition to Password Policy, I also configured the following Group Policy settings in the same Active Directory home lab:

1. Account Lockout Policy
Configured account lockout settings to protect the domain from brute-force login attempts.

2. Desktop Wallpaper Policy
Configured a default desktop wallpaper for users.

3. Control Panel Restriction
Restricted users from accessing the Control Panel.

4. USB Storage Restriction
Configured policy settings to prevent the use of removable USB storage devices.

5. Drive Mapping
Configured user drive mapping settings for shared resources.

These additional policies were implemented to simulate common enterprise system administration and security tasks.

